	<h3>Matching cost does not grow with subscriptions</h3>
	<p>
		All active subscriptions live in an in-memory Trie. Lookup time is proportional to the length of the topic, not the
		number of subscriptions — a broker holding a million subscriptions matches a topic as fast as one holding a
		thousand. Adding subscribers does not slow down routing.
	</p>

	<h3>Shared subscriptions that add throughput</h3>
	<p>
		Mosquitto implements MQTT 5.0 shared subscriptions and so does TBMQ, so this is not a support difference — it is a
		difference in what adding a member does. Register an
		<a href="/docs/application-shared-subscription/">application shared subscription</a> and the group gets its own Kafka
		topic; each subscribing APPLICATION client joins that topic's consumer group, so an extra instance picks up a share of
		the partitions and the group rebalances when one dies. Messages keep accumulating in Kafka while every member is offline,
		and delivery resumes from the committed offset.
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/shared-subscriptions/">Shared subscriptions</a> covers the DEVICE-client behavior and the load-balancing
		strategies.
	</p>

	<h3>The running cluster is inspectable</h3>
	<p>
		Mosquitto's operational surface is a log file and the <code>$SYS</code> topic tree. TBMQ answers the same questions from
		a <a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/monitoring/">web UI</a> over the live cluster: which
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/sessions/">sessions</a> are connected and what each is subscribed to, per-client and cluster-wide
		metrics, which connection attempts were rejected and why, and a built-in
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/websocket-client/">WebSocket client</a> for publishing a test message without leaving the
		browser. The same objects are reachable over the <a href="/docs/rest-api/">REST API</a>, and metrics export to
		<a href="/docs/prometheus-metrics/">Prometheus</a>.
	</p>

	<p>
		All of that is Community Edition, <a href="https://github.com/thingsboard/tbmq">Apache 2.0</a>, commercial use
		included. <a href="/pricing/">Professional Edition</a> adds OAuth 2.0 single sign-on, role-based access control, audit
		logs, domain management and white labeling — on the same broker, not a different one.
	</p>

	<h2>What the architecture is built to do</h2>
	<p>Each figure comes from a published test, with its hardware and load profile documented.</p>

	<LearnIconRows>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:arrows-split-2" title="3M msg/sec">
			fan-out from a single 32-vCPU node — 100 publishers to 3,000 subscribers at QoS 0, 7.4 ms average latency, 54%
			CPU.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:plug-connected" title="100M connections">
			held on a 25-node cluster across 100M unique topics, while moving 6M messages per second at QoS 1.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:arrows-right-left" title="1M msg/sec point-to-point">
			500,000 publishers to 500,000 persistent device subscribers at QoS 1, on five nodes.
		</LearnIconRow>
	</LearnIconRows>

	<p>
		One caveat, stated plainly: <strong>Mosquitto publishes no comparable figures</strong> — its homepage makes no numeric
		connection or throughput claim at all. These are TBMQ tests on TBMQ hardware, not a same-hardware comparison, and they
		should not be read as one.
	</p>
	<p><a href="/performance/">Full benchmark methodology and results →</a></p>

	<h2>At a glance</h2>
	<div class="overflow-x">
