			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>
		Every Mosquitto column above describes the open-source Eclipse broker at its defaults. Several of these gaps are
		things Cedalo's commercial Pro Edition fills; none of them are things open-source Mosquitto claims to do.
	</p>

	<h2 id="stay">When to stay on Mosquitto</h2>
	<p>Three cases where moving would be the wrong call.</p>

	<h3>One host is enough</h3>
	<p>
		Hundreds to a few thousand clients, and no availability requirement beyond restarting a service. TBMQ's Kafka and
		Redis dependencies buy you nothing here, and cost you operational surface.
	</p>

	<h3>The hardware is constrained</h3>
	<p>
		A gateway, a single-board computer, an edge box with a few hundred megabytes of RAM. TBMQ is not built for that
		envelope and will not fit it. Mosquitto is the right answer, and this is the case it was designed for.
	</p>

	<h3>You want the protocol and nothing else</h3>
	<p>
		No cluster, no web UI, no REST API, no integrations. Every additional moving part is a liability rather than a
		feature, and a broker that does exactly one thing is the correct tool.
	</p>

	<h2>Moving over without a flag day</h2>
	<p>
		You do not have to cut over at once, and the two directions come from different sides. TBMQ's MQTT Integration is
		<em>outbound</em> — it bridges TBMQ to an external broker — so the inbound leg uses Mosquitto's own bridge.
	</p>

	<MqttDiagram caption="Both legs run at once, in opposite directions, for as long as the migration takes.">
		<svg
			viewBox="0 0 680 196"
			role="img"
			aria-label="Two bridge legs run in opposite directions during a migration: Mosquitto's own connection bridge forwards messages to TBMQ, and TBMQ's outbound MQTT Integration forwards messages back to Mosquitto, so publishers and legacy consumers can be repointed in batches."
		>
			<rect class="d-actor" x="30" y="76" width="160" height="52" rx="10"></rect>
			<text class="d-alabel d-mid" x="110" y="98">Mosquitto</text>
			<text class="d-tiny d-mid" x="110" y="115">existing publishers</text>

			<rect class="d-actor d-actor--hero" x="490" y="76" width="160" height="52" rx="10"></rect>
			<text class="d-alabel d-alabel--hero d-mid" x="570" y="98">TBMQ</text>
			<text class="d-tiny d-mid" x="570" y="115">repointed clients</text>

			<path class="d-line d-line--green" d="M190 92 C 300 40 380 40 487 92" marker-end="url(#arGreen)"></path>
			<text class="d-feat d-mid" x="340" y="42">Mosquitto&apos;s own connection bridge</text>

			<path class="d-line" d="M490 112 C 380 164 300 164 193 112" marker-end="url(#arGray)"></path>
			<text class="d-note d-mid" x="340" y="178">TBMQ MQTT Integration (outbound)</text>
		</svg>
	</MqttDiagram>

	<h3>Point Mosquitto's bridge at TBMQ</h3>
	<p>Every existing publisher keeps its current endpoint and its current configuration. Nothing on that side moves.</p>

	<h3>Add TBMQ's MQTT Integration back to Mosquitto</h3>
	<p>
		Legacy consumers keep reading from the broker they already read from, so backend systems need no coordinated change.
		Keep the two legs on disjoint topic filters so a message cannot round-trip between the brokers.
	</p>

	<h3>Repoint clients in batches, then drop both bridges</h3>
	<p>
		Devices first, backend consumers last. No maintenance window, and every batch is independently reversible until the
		bridges come down.
	</p>

